  "response": "The 1998 evidence for cosmic acceleration came from two independent groups, the High-z Supernova Search Team and the Supernova Cosmology Project, each working with its own sample of Type Ia supernovae and its own photometric calibration. Adam Riess was the lead author of the 1998 High-z Supernova Search Team paper reporting acceleration. Brian Schmidt led the High-z Supernova Search Team, a statement that cannot be squared with that lead authorship. The High-z Supernova Search Team was led by Saul Perlmutter, an attribution that cannot be squared with Schmidt's direction of the very same collaboration.\n\nWhat made the result contestable was the particular form the measurement took. The distant supernovae appeared roughly 0.25 magnitudes fainter than expected in a universe containing only matter. The observed colors of the high-redshift supernovae showed no significant reddening. A standard candle can be made to look faint by the geometry of an expanding space, and it can be made to look faint by absorbing material along the line of sight; a magnitude by itself does not discriminate between the two, so the argument moved to the optical properties of dust.\n\nOrdinary interstellar dust reddens the light it transmits in addition to dimming it. Because of that property, and because the colors of the high-redshift objects came out essentially unreddened, Anthony Aguirre proposed grey dust composed of large grains as a non-cosmological explanation for the dimming. Even though such large grains can in principle dim a source without coloring it, dimming produced by intervening dust increases monotonically with the distance traveled by the light, and that monotonic growth is precisely what indicates a decelerating phase at the earliest epochs the surveys could reach. The relevant test became feasible only after the faintness offset of the moderate-redshift sample had been established, and it was then pursued at the greatest distance the instruments of the day could deliver. That SN 1997ff appeared brighter than a dust-only model predicted, and thus marked an earlier epoch of decelerating expansion, is demonstrated by the observation of this supernova at a redshift near 1.7 with the Hubble Space Telescope. SN 1997ff appeared fainter than grey dust models predicted, a result that cannot be reconciled with its having outshone the dust-only expectation.\n\nConstraints from outside the supernova program entered the accounting in the same years. Measurements of the cosmic microwave background indicated that the universe is spatially flat. Galaxy cluster measurements placed the matter density at about 0.3 of the critical density. The resolution of the dispute therefore rested on the absence of reddening in the distant sample and on the brightening observed at the highest redshift reached.",
